    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Right unit.** The blocklist data (`DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS`) and the domain-matching logic (`isDisposableDomain`) land in `OwnerEmail`, which already declares itself the \"single source of truth for the owner email rule.\" The predicate sits beside its siblings `isValid` and `normalize`, and the class Javadoc was updated to reflect the added responsibility. This is exactly where a maintainer would expect to find it.\n- **Follows the established pattern.** Each validator already normalized the email, then did an `if (email != null && !OwnerEmail.isValid(...)) throw new InvalidEmailException(...)`. The new disposable-domain check is a parallel block in the same shape, reusing the same exception type and message convention. It reads as a natural continuation rather than a bolt-on.\n- **No new catch-all.** `OwnerEmail` stays a focused, cohesive helper; the validators gain three lines each and no new responsibility.\n- **Duplication is acceptable and pre-existing.** The check block appears in both `ValidateOwner` and `ValidateNewOwner`, but the *logic* is centralized in `OwnerEmail` \u2014 only the throw/message is repeated, mirroring the existing `isValid` duplication. The design deliberately keeps exception-throwing per-pipeline (the message interpolates `request.getEmail()` and escalation is per-endpoint), so folding it into a single throwing method would be a larger refactor beyond this task and against the existing grain.\n- The defensive null / missing-`@` handling in `isDisposableDomain` is harmless and clearly documented as belt-and-suspenders given the callers' guarantees.\n\nNo changes needed.",
